00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.530
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:06.380
Welcome, you all,

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.823
to our monthly Nature of
Inference colloquium series.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.260
As you all know, this is a new series

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.060
that we have started
at the Franke Program,

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.430
motivated by a lot of my own work.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.790
Those of you who don't know
me, I'm Priya Natarajan.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:24.130
I am an astrophysicist,

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.700
and I happen to be the current director

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.270
of the Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities,

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.700
at Yale.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.580
My own work is, has been
in trying to understand

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.938
the phenomenology of black
holes in the universe,

00:00:35.938 --> 00:00:38.110
and a persistent issue,

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:41.680
that has nagged all the modeling
and conceptual work I do,

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.570
is, whether the correlations,
empirical correlations,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.137
that we actually see between
properties of black holes,

00:00:48.137 --> 00:00:50.350
and the galaxies that
host them, and so on,

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.680
whether they actually
reflect a causal physics,

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.560
or are they just happenstance,

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.540
a consequence of some kind
of statistical manifestation

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.390
of the evolution of structure formation.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.800
So that, sort of, seeded this interest,

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.970
but I've been so fortunate
that, as part of the series,

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:08.840
we have invited

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.810
very distinguished researchers
across disciplines,

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.710
who are working on
understanding the complex issues

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:16.490
of causal inference.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.520
Focusing on how we draw robust conclusions

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.640
from conceptual models, and simulations.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.160
So, the series represents
cutting-edge researchers,

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.410
who are exploring various
kinds of conceptual frameworks,

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:31.650
agent simulations,

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.650
ab initio time-evolution modeling

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.890
of complex nonlinear systems,

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.000
propagation of genetic
transcription and expression,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.133
climate-modeling,

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.650
to distill the role that
modeling actually plays

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.480
in helping disentangle
correlation and causation.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:48.470
And so we are getting this,

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.490
we're gonna to get this
multi-disciplinary view,

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:52.630
of how this is done.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.600
It's a very challenging problem,

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.750
and it's a very fundamental
problem in epistemology,

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.950
across intellectual disciplines,

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:03.080
this distinction between
causation and correlation.

