00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:24.190
where I assume you're making
that assumption, right?

00:41:24.190 --> 00:41:26.190
- [Nihat] Yeah, so
that's a very good point,

00:41:26.190 --> 00:41:28.150
I'm actually not...

00:41:28.150 --> 00:41:31.920
That's partly true, and it's
actually a very good point.

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:33.940
So some-

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:35.900
so you could think,

00:41:35.900 --> 00:41:40.280
that, in order to make this,
to to have this equation,

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:42.100
you need to assume,

00:41:42.100 --> 00:41:45.470
so to say, to know the
structure of the underlying,

00:41:45.470 --> 00:41:48.310
the underlying structure of the network.

00:41:48.310 --> 00:41:49.850
The point I'm want to make here,

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:52.073
is that we don't need that, actually.

00:41:53.120 --> 00:41:56.090
No matter how the surrounding network is,

00:41:56.090 --> 00:41:58.363
you don't need any information about this.

00:42:00.610 --> 00:42:02.210
The only information that you need,

00:42:02.210 --> 00:42:04.540
but that might be even too much,

00:42:04.540 --> 00:42:08.340
is what are the parents of v?

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:11.030
So, only knowing the joint distribution-

00:42:11.030 --> 00:42:14.900
so, you do need to know the parents of v,

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:17.860
so, which is, in this case, just i and j.

00:42:17.860 --> 00:42:20.610
In the setting of, of neurons, of course,

00:42:20.610 --> 00:42:23.473
you have like 1,000 or 10,000 neurons,

00:42:24.370 --> 00:42:26.730
connected to one particular neuron.

00:42:26.730 --> 00:42:29.023
So you would have to
record all the parents,

00:42:29.900 --> 00:42:30.733
but-

00:42:32.410 --> 00:42:34.383
the brain is, of course, much more.

00:42:35.512 --> 00:42:36.540
So, the point I want to make

00:42:36.540 --> 00:42:38.183
is that local information,

00:42:40.350 --> 00:42:42.560
related to V, is enough,

00:42:42.560 --> 00:42:47.250
in order to predict the
outcome of intervention,

00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:48.163
in v,

00:42:50.330 --> 00:42:52.270
with respect to w,

00:42:52.270 --> 00:42:56.443
no matter what structure is around that-

00:42:58.080 --> 00:42:59.420
that triple here,

00:42:59.420 --> 00:43:01.520
so that you don't need more information.

00:43:01.520 --> 00:43:04.480
You need some part of it, that's
I think the benefit of it.

00:43:04.480 --> 00:43:05.960
- [Kyle] I know, I agree.
- So it's really

00:43:05.960 --> 00:43:06.800
a full-

00:43:06.800 --> 00:43:09.410
it's really a full chicken/egg problem,

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:12.820
if you would assume to
know the whole network,

00:43:12.820 --> 00:43:13.860
but the main point here,

00:43:13.860 --> 00:43:15.977
is that you don't need to
know the whole network,

00:43:15.977 --> 00:43:19.563
but you do need to know
something about the network.

00:43:20.963 --> 00:43:21.796
Does that answer

00:43:21.796 --> 00:43:22.629
your question?
- [Kyle] Did you call this

00:43:22.629 --> 00:43:23.530
conditional... Yeah, I agree (indistinct)

00:43:23.530 --> 00:43:26.630
And this conditional, on the right,

00:43:26.630 --> 00:43:28.600
would you consider that local information?

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:30.010
It seems like, in the picture, you know,

00:43:30.010 --> 00:43:32.130
w and v are quite distinct.

00:43:32.130 --> 00:43:34.050
But I agree, it's certainly
not knowing the whole network.

00:43:34.050 --> 00:43:34.883
It's a,

00:43:36.150 --> 00:43:37.630
it's a much smaller amount of information,

00:43:37.630 --> 00:43:40.025
than you can...
- Yeah. That's much smaller.

00:43:40.025 --> 00:43:44.090
So of course, in my drawing,

00:43:44.090 --> 00:43:46.570
I wanted to highlight how small it is,

00:43:46.570 --> 00:43:48.183
so I have just two parents here,

00:43:49.890 --> 00:43:51.570
two nodes as parents,

00:43:51.570 --> 00:43:53.050
but-

00:43:53.050 --> 00:43:54.885
sometimes it is feasible.

00:43:54.885 --> 00:43:59.080
You could record from the
parents, and the node itself.

00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:02.090
So you have to record
from the parents of E,

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:05.350
and so this is the joint
distribution that you need, here.

00:44:05.350 --> 00:44:06.813
And if you don't have access to that,

00:44:06.813 --> 00:44:09.200
there's no way to compute it.

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:11.710
Okay. But it's much, much less

00:44:11.710 --> 00:44:14.703
than knowing the full joint distribution.

00:44:16.410 --> 00:44:17.950
- [Kyle] Great. Thank you.

00:44:17.950 --> 00:44:19.200
- You are welcome.

00:44:19.200 --> 00:44:20.083
So that's-

00:44:20.960 --> 00:44:21.793
one thing,

00:44:21.793 --> 00:44:24.040
that's the backdoor criterion,

00:44:24.040 --> 00:44:27.510
and the front-door criterion
is somewhat surprising.

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:28.703
More surprising,

00:44:30.170 --> 00:44:33.740
if you had this network here, let's say.

00:44:33.740 --> 00:44:35.860
So the way I think about it is,

00:44:35.860 --> 00:44:40.810
so I have, let's say these are
like intrinsic observations,

00:44:40.810 --> 00:44:43.990
of a system, intrinsic
variables of system,

00:44:43.990 --> 00:44:48.583
and then do you have U as the
huge environment, let's say.

00:44:51.323 --> 00:44:53.400
So it's acting on A and C,

00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:56.350
so this is a common cause of A and C,

00:44:56.350 --> 00:45:00.460
and it seems that there is no
way to actually compute the-

00:45:00.460 --> 00:45:03.890
to predict the effect of intervention

00:45:03.890 --> 00:45:06.060
in A on C,

00:45:06.060 --> 00:45:09.320
without having the full information here.

00:45:09.320 --> 00:45:11.840
So let's say an agent is really restricted

00:45:11.840 --> 00:45:15.967
to observing A, B and C,
and then it's affected by U,

00:45:15.967 --> 00:45:17.980
and the only requirement
that you have here,

00:45:17.980 --> 00:45:20.080
is that this intermediate variable

00:45:20.080 --> 00:45:22.100
is not affected by the environment.

00:45:22.100 --> 00:45:23.680
So just A and C.

00:45:23.680 --> 00:45:25.883
But the way it's set up here, is-

00:45:27.580 --> 00:45:28.760
to actually demonstrate

00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:30.510
that it could be quite counter-intuitive.

00:45:30.510 --> 00:45:31.510
So, in this case,

00:45:31.510 --> 00:45:34.570
I wouldn't expect to be able to compute

00:45:34.570 --> 00:45:38.749
the effect of intervention in A, and on C,

00:45:38.749 --> 00:45:40.499
(stammering)

00:45:40.499 --> 00:45:43.420
based on just observing A, B and C.

00:45:43.420 --> 00:45:44.470
But, it turns out,

00:45:44.470 --> 00:45:46.653
that if you go through the computation,

00:45:47.630 --> 00:45:48.463
it's...

00:45:49.520 --> 00:45:52.050
Here's a trick, you have to-

00:45:52.050 --> 00:45:55.130
you have to use some
condition-independent statements here,

00:45:55.130 --> 00:45:57.103
but then you end up with something.

00:45:58.600 --> 00:46:00.600
So the interesting part is, this,

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:01.940
so you start with something

00:46:01.940 --> 00:46:04.210
that takes into account
the mechanism of U.

00:46:04.210 --> 00:46:06.740
This huge environment, let's say,

00:46:06.740 --> 00:46:09.140
or surrounding world,

00:46:09.140 --> 00:46:13.408
you have this mechanism
that has this, u, u, u,

00:46:13.408 --> 00:46:17.030
everywhere, and suddenly,
in this last line,

00:46:17.030 --> 00:46:20.110
u cancels out, and there is no u anymore.

00:46:20.110 --> 00:46:23.410
And this simply just uses
condition-independent statements

00:46:23.410 --> 00:46:24.690
that are compatible,

00:46:24.690 --> 00:46:26.860
follow from this causal structure, here.

00:46:26.860 --> 00:46:29.700
So this says that if you observe,

00:46:29.700 --> 00:46:32.023
just A, B and C,

00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:35.160
let's call them intrinsic variables,

00:46:35.160 --> 00:46:38.590
you can predict the effect of,

00:46:38.590 --> 00:46:41.570
of intervention in A on C,

00:46:41.570 --> 00:46:44.430
without actually
intervening into the system.

00:46:44.430 --> 00:46:46.730
And that's, to me, it's quite surprising.

00:46:46.730 --> 00:46:49.580
It's more surprising
than even the, the front,

00:46:49.580 --> 00:46:51.403
the backdoor criteria.

00:46:53.150 --> 00:46:54.563
Just as a-

00:46:56.600 --> 00:46:59.360
as an application,

00:46:59.360 --> 00:47:01.913
so we did apply this to,

00:47:02.990 --> 00:47:04.310
in the robotics context,

00:47:04.310 --> 00:47:08.090
so coming back to maybe one comment here,

00:47:08.090 --> 00:47:10.910
I started with directed acyclic graphs,

00:47:10.910 --> 00:47:13.460
often, of course, like in neural networks,

00:47:13.460 --> 00:47:16.280
or in agents interacting with the world,

00:47:16.280 --> 00:47:17.650
we have cycles,

00:47:17.650 --> 00:47:20.670
but the way we interpret them,

00:47:20.670 --> 00:47:22.270
that is-

00:47:22.270 --> 00:47:24.470
by unfolding them in time,

00:47:24.470 --> 00:47:27.240
so you get this directed acyclic graph,

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:29.330
which represents the world,

00:47:29.330 --> 00:47:33.900
and the brain of the agent
interacting with the world,

00:47:33.900 --> 00:47:36.963
through sensing and acting, okay?

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:41.970
We actually simulate this kind of a-

00:47:41.970 --> 00:47:43.980
of a sensory motor-loop,

00:47:43.980 --> 00:47:47.540
in the context of agents that you create,

00:47:47.540 --> 00:47:50.550
within the setting of virtual robotics.

00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:51.923
So this is a-

00:47:54.550 --> 00:47:58.160
kind of a creature that
we, for example, analyze.

00:47:58.160 --> 00:48:01.010
Then interesting question would be here,

00:48:01.010 --> 00:48:03.250
and that relates also
to the previous talk,

00:48:03.250 --> 00:48:05.590
to the notion of agency.

00:48:05.590 --> 00:48:08.830
So what is, what is the,

00:48:08.830 --> 00:48:12.830
we could ask, what is the cause
and effect of the actions?

00:48:12.830 --> 00:48:17.350
So this basically
summarizes the whole past,

00:48:17.350 --> 00:48:19.890
the history to one variable age,

00:48:19.890 --> 00:48:21.690
and reduces,

00:48:21.690 --> 00:48:22.690
so to say,

00:48:22.690 --> 00:48:24.400
the whole sensory,

00:48:24.400 --> 00:48:27.860
the whole causal diagram
of the sensory motor-loop,

00:48:27.860 --> 00:48:29.253
to this small, simpler one.

00:48:30.130 --> 00:48:32.920
So now you can ask, what
is the causal effect of A

00:48:32.920 --> 00:48:36.910
on the next of the actions
of the next sensory input?

00:48:36.910 --> 00:48:39.090
And when we started this,

00:48:39.090 --> 00:48:40.280
I was...

00:48:41.931 --> 00:48:45.940
And we thought, okay, we have
this mechanism of the world,

00:48:45.940 --> 00:48:49.790
which is, even in a
simulation like this, it,

00:48:49.790 --> 00:48:50.960
you cannot deal with it,

00:48:50.960 --> 00:48:52.800
It has too many states, you don't...

00:48:52.800 --> 00:48:55.630
And that's basically
what the physics engine,

00:48:55.630 --> 00:48:59.190
alpha is basically implemented
within this physics engine,

00:48:59.190 --> 00:49:00.890
and then you have the sensors,

00:49:00.890 --> 00:49:02.888
so there's no way to compute it

00:49:02.888 --> 00:49:06.210
by incorporating alpha and beta.

00:49:06.210 --> 00:49:09.163
But then we realized,
actually we don't need that.

00:49:12.970 --> 00:49:14.560
So, if you'll study it carefully,

00:49:14.560 --> 00:49:18.673
the causal effect of the
actions on the sensors,

00:49:19.820 --> 00:49:21.913
is identifiable,

00:49:22.950 --> 00:49:24.640
intrinsically by the agent,

00:49:24.640 --> 00:49:25.880
by just measuring

00:49:25.880 --> 00:49:29.180
the joint distribution
of its brain-state, C,

00:49:29.180 --> 00:49:32.890
it's activator states A,
and the next sensor-states.

00:49:32.890 --> 00:49:35.500
So the left-hand side is
interventional in nature,

00:49:35.500 --> 00:49:37.550
and the right hand side is observational.

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:42.250
And that's simply by
the backdoor criterion.

00:49:42.250 --> 00:49:44.910
And you can actually use
the front-door criterion

00:49:44.910 --> 00:49:47.780
to compute the causal effect of your brain

00:49:47.780 --> 00:49:49.740
on the next sensor-state.

00:49:49.740 --> 00:49:52.250
Again, we have an
interventional thing here,

00:49:52.250 --> 00:49:53.133
interventional-

00:49:55.030 --> 00:49:56.300
a notion on the left-hand side,

00:49:56.300 --> 00:49:57.730
and observational notion here.

00:49:57.730 --> 00:49:59.823
So we could let the robot simply-

00:50:01.410 --> 00:50:04.020
run, and do something,

00:50:04.020 --> 00:50:07.060
and it will record a joint distribution,

00:50:07.060 --> 00:50:09.800
and then predict its causal effects

00:50:09.800 --> 00:50:11.630
on the next sensor-input.

00:50:11.630 --> 00:50:13.187
So,

00:50:13.187 --> 00:50:14.980
the shortcoming here,

00:50:14.980 --> 00:50:17.510
and I want to highlight this,

00:50:17.510 --> 00:50:20.730
all these equations are
based on the assumption

00:50:20.730 --> 00:50:24.643
that the probability distributions
are strictly positive.

00:50:25.730 --> 00:50:27.610
Pearl is assuming this,

00:50:27.610 --> 00:50:30.870
and identifiability of cause and effects

00:50:30.870 --> 00:50:33.810
always starts with this assumption.

00:50:33.810 --> 00:50:37.770
In robotic settings, we have
a functional dependencies,

00:50:37.770 --> 00:50:39.100
and this-

00:50:39.100 --> 00:50:40.660
this assumption is not met.

00:50:40.660 --> 00:50:43.430
This is why we are actually
working on generalizations

00:50:43.430 --> 00:50:45.887
of this kind of identifiability.

00:50:47.760 --> 00:50:50.473
So that's just one application.

00:50:52.870 --> 00:50:54.640
Let me come, maybe...

00:50:54.640 --> 00:50:57.770
So how much time do I have?

00:50:57.770 --> 00:50:58.760
Left?

00:50:58.760 --> 00:51:01.884
- [Priya] You could go for
another 5 to 10 minutes,

00:51:01.884 --> 00:51:02.780
if you wanted to

00:51:02.780 --> 00:51:04.030
- [Nihat] Okay.
- and wrap up.

00:51:04.030 --> 00:51:05.640
- Let me highlight the
Common Cause Principle,

00:51:05.640 --> 00:51:07.920
because I think this is
really, really important.

00:51:07.920 --> 00:51:12.320
So, we have seen that
observation is not enough,

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:14.810
so we have to intervene sometimes,

00:51:14.810 --> 00:51:18.070
in order to get the
structure out of the system.

00:51:18.070 --> 00:51:20.010
And then, if we cannot intervene,

00:51:20.010 --> 00:51:22.650
we can actually identify, sometimes,

00:51:22.650 --> 00:51:26.170
the causal effect, even
without actual intervention,

00:51:26.170 --> 00:51:28.790
by so-called virtual intervention,

00:51:28.790 --> 00:51:32.043
replacing the intervention by observation.

00:51:33.080 --> 00:51:36.640
But that, as pointed out correctly,

00:51:36.640 --> 00:51:39.120
that requires some additional information

00:51:39.120 --> 00:51:40.520
about the underlying structure.

00:51:40.520 --> 00:51:42.790
Like knowing the parents, for example.

00:51:42.790 --> 00:51:44.190
You don't have to know everything,

00:51:44.190 --> 00:51:46.680
but you have to know something.

00:51:46.680 --> 00:51:49.040
And, sometimes, you even don't know that.

00:51:49.040 --> 00:51:50.690
So you know, don't know anything.

00:51:51.970 --> 00:51:54.870
This is why I got interested
in the common cause principle.

00:51:54.870 --> 00:51:56.370
Even without knowing anything,

00:51:56.370 --> 00:52:00.850
you can say something about the
underlying causal structure,

00:52:00.850 --> 00:52:02.573
and that brings me to the-

00:52:03.610 --> 00:52:05.820
to the common cause principle.

00:52:05.820 --> 00:52:09.540
Let me first introduce what,

00:52:09.540 --> 00:52:12.270
how we model,

00:52:12.270 --> 00:52:15.640
how we quantify stochastic dependence.

00:52:15.640 --> 00:52:17.110
So, basically,

00:52:17.110 --> 00:52:20.853
I really like this
information-geometry quantification.

00:52:21.760 --> 00:52:23.480
Stochastic dependence, basically,

00:52:23.480 --> 00:52:28.010
compares a joint
distribution, of variables,

00:52:28.010 --> 00:52:30.710
from the product of the marginals.

00:52:30.710 --> 00:52:35.320
Okay. And you have independence,
if this equals this, here.

00:52:35.320 --> 00:52:37.303
So if you have equality,

00:52:38.320 --> 00:52:39.210
this is a 1,

00:52:40.440 --> 00:52:43.390
so this becomes 1, and
logarithm of 1 is 0,

00:52:43.390 --> 00:52:45.640
so the whole thing becomes 0.

00:52:45.640 --> 00:52:46.920
So, basically,

00:52:46.920 --> 00:52:51.920
a stochastic dependence is the
distance from independence,

00:52:52.300 --> 00:52:55.820
and by distance, I mean
the (indistinct) distance

00:52:55.820 --> 00:52:57.300
or (indistinct) divergence,

00:52:57.300 --> 00:52:59.410
which is this quantity here.

00:52:59.410 --> 00:53:02.000
So, and here is a visualization.

00:53:02.000 --> 00:53:03.950
So this manifold here,

00:53:03.950 --> 00:53:05.980
this surface,

00:53:05.980 --> 00:53:08.960
is basically denoting the
set of all distributions

00:53:08.960 --> 00:53:11.170
that have product structure,

00:53:11.170 --> 00:53:12.420
and,

00:53:12.420 --> 00:53:14.060
dependence is simp-

00:53:14.060 --> 00:53:17.090
dependence of a joint distribution p,

00:53:17.090 --> 00:53:18.960
in a joint distribution p,

00:53:18.960 --> 00:53:22.860
is quantified as distance
from independence.

00:53:22.860 --> 00:53:26.540
And this distance is referred
to as multi-information,

00:53:26.540 --> 00:53:29.730
and in this case, where
you have two variables,

00:53:29.730 --> 00:53:33.110
so n equals 2, it's simply
the mutual information.

00:53:33.110 --> 00:53:35.230
So that's a nice geometric interpretation

00:53:35.230 --> 00:53:37.123
of stochastic dependence.

00:53:38.469 --> 00:53:42.210
Now I want to present
a quite strong theorem,

00:53:42.210 --> 00:53:44.170
that we derived, but for that,

00:53:44.170 --> 00:53:47.758
I need to introduce the
notion of a common cause.

00:53:47.758 --> 00:53:49.680
Let's say we have a network,

00:53:49.680 --> 00:53:53.008
and we have observed
variables, here denoted by,

00:53:53.008 --> 00:53:55.420
in green, here, highlighted in green.

00:53:55.420 --> 00:53:57.330
So these are the observed variables,

00:53:57.330 --> 00:53:58.973
and we have some surrounding,

00:53:59.900 --> 00:54:00.993
causal network.

00:54:02.310 --> 00:54:04.220
And, Ac-

00:54:04.220 --> 00:54:05.790
denotes-

00:54:05.790 --> 00:54:07.120
the-

00:54:07.120 --> 00:54:11.200
set of elements, in the network,

00:54:11.200 --> 00:54:12.950
in the full network,

00:54:12.950 --> 00:54:15.200
that reach, at least,

00:54:15.200 --> 00:54:17.833
C-observed variables.

00:54:18.920 --> 00:54:19.753
Okay?

00:54:19.753 --> 00:54:21.083
For example, A2,

00:54:22.250 --> 00:54:25.840
is this, so this is denoted by red dots,

00:54:25.840 --> 00:54:27.750
here, red nodes.

00:54:27.750 --> 00:54:31.850
So all the red ones, reach at least two-

00:54:31.850 --> 00:54:33.260
observed variables.

00:54:33.260 --> 00:54:36.120
For example, this one reaches this,

00:54:36.120 --> 00:54:37.970
and this.

00:54:37.970 --> 00:54:39.300
Okay?

00:54:39.300 --> 00:54:41.343
This one is reached,

00:54:42.560 --> 00:54:43.640
reaches also 2,

00:54:43.640 --> 00:54:45.600
it reaches this observed one,

00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:49.940
but it also reaches itself,
by a path of length 0.

00:54:49.940 --> 00:54:51.780
That's an important point here.

00:54:51.780 --> 00:54:54.403
We refer to this as the
set of common ancestors.

00:54:56.540 --> 00:54:58.903
So that's this, A stands for ancestors,

00:54:59.830 --> 00:55:02.260
and the important point here is,

00:55:02.260 --> 00:55:05.400
that when you observe
the joint distribution,

00:55:05.400 --> 00:55:07.683
so you have n observed variables,

00:55:08.580 --> 00:55:10.134
and-

00:55:10.134 --> 00:55:12.110
you, basically,

00:55:12.110 --> 00:55:13.250
quantify,

00:55:13.250 --> 00:55:15.860
there correlation in this way.

00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:17.723
H denotes the entropy.

00:55:19.040 --> 00:55:21.950
So you take the sum of marginal entropies,

00:55:21.950 --> 00:55:25.370
and subtract C times-

00:55:25.370 --> 00:55:27.970
the joint entropy.

00:55:27.970 --> 00:55:31.700
For C=1, it's simply this,

00:55:31.700 --> 00:55:33.780
this quantity here.

00:55:33.780 --> 00:55:36.830
For C=1, it is simply
the multi-information.

00:55:36.830 --> 00:55:41.320
So, that will be greater than
or equal to 0 anyway, for C=1.

00:55:41.320 --> 00:55:42.710
But,

00:55:42.710 --> 00:55:45.430
so the theorem that we could,

00:55:45.430 --> 00:55:46.263
that we could...

00:55:46.263 --> 00:55:48.670
Actually, the inequality
that we could prove,

00:55:48.670 --> 00:55:49.583
is the following.

00:55:50.680 --> 00:55:52.750
You can upper-bound,

00:55:52.750 --> 00:55:57.750
ah, lower bound the entropy
of the common ancestors,

00:55:57.930 --> 00:56:00.570
of degree C+1,

00:56:00.570 --> 00:56:02.660
by this quantity here,

00:56:02.660 --> 00:56:07.660
quantity of correlation, only
of the observed variables.

00:56:08.200 --> 00:56:09.700
So, again,

00:56:09.700 --> 00:56:13.910
the right-hand side is
something that can be computed,

00:56:13.910 --> 00:56:16.440
based on the observed variables.

00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:19.210
The left-hand side really requires

00:56:19.210 --> 00:56:21.510
the full surrounding network,

00:56:21.510 --> 00:56:24.123
and it's the entropy of
the common ancestors.

00:56:25.590 --> 00:56:28.530
So, which means that if
you observe n variables,

00:56:28.530 --> 00:56:29.390
that you quantif...

00:56:29.390 --> 00:56:33.150
You evaluate this quantity,

00:56:33.150 --> 00:56:36.850
and it it turns out to be positive,

00:56:36.850 --> 00:56:38.620
then you can infer-

00:56:38.620 --> 00:56:42.023
that there is at least
one common ancestor,

00:56:43.098 --> 00:56:46.583
of at least C+1 observed variables.

00:56:47.970 --> 00:56:50.410
So you can infer the existence

00:56:50.410 --> 00:56:53.470
of common ancestors of observed variables,

00:56:53.470 --> 00:56:54.650
because, otherwise,

00:56:54.650 --> 00:56:56.380
it's not possible to explain

00:56:56.380 --> 00:57:00.020
the correlation of the observed variables.

00:57:00.020 --> 00:57:01.180
Here's an example,

00:57:01.180 --> 00:57:05.410
the famous Reichenbach's
common cause principle.

00:57:05.410 --> 00:57:08.900
If you just plug in C=1, here,

00:57:08.900 --> 00:57:10.240
it tells you, basically,

00:57:10.240 --> 00:57:13.420
that's a corollary of
this general theorem,

00:57:13.420 --> 00:57:17.780
if two observed variables are dependent,

00:57:17.780 --> 00:57:20.830
then, so these are the two variables,

00:57:20.830 --> 00:57:23.520
then there must be a path from 1 to 2,

00:57:23.520 --> 00:57:24.910
a path from 2 to 1,

00:57:24.910 --> 00:57:29.300
or there must be a common
cause, of both of them.

00:57:29.300 --> 00:57:31.040
So that's a simple implication

00:57:31.040 --> 00:57:34.227
of the more general theorem that we have.

00:57:34.227 --> 00:57:36.560
Here's another example.

00:57:36.560 --> 00:57:38.403
If you have variables-

00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:41.770
that are completely correlate,

00:57:41.770 --> 00:57:46.770
maximally correlated,
then, you cannot explai-

00:57:46.860 --> 00:57:50.573
then there must be a common
cause of all of them.

00:57:52.270 --> 00:57:53.530
So that's the implication.

00:57:53.530 --> 00:57:56.180
So, this kind of correlation,
complete correlation,

00:57:56.180 --> 00:57:58.750
can only be achieved by a common cause,

00:57:58.750 --> 00:58:03.110
or common ancestor, that
reaches all observed nodes.

00:58:03.110 --> 00:58:05.060
This already excludes, for example,

00:58:05.060 --> 00:58:06.960
if you compare these networks,

00:58:06.960 --> 00:58:08.390
you can say this is possible,

00:58:08.390 --> 00:58:10.913
because this reaches all of them,

00:58:11.760 --> 00:58:14.720
and here, it's also reached this one.

00:58:14.720 --> 00:58:15.960
This is also possible,

00:58:15.960 --> 00:58:17.410
but these two are not possible.

00:58:17.410 --> 00:58:21.610
So you could exclude potential
explanations of the data,

00:58:21.610 --> 00:58:24.503
simply based on the
correlation-structure of the nodes.

00:58:25.650 --> 00:58:29.033
And here again, by this kind of test,

00:58:29.870 --> 00:58:30.703
you-

00:58:32.040 --> 00:58:36.910
if you compare the sum of
of the Shannon entropies

00:58:36.910 --> 00:58:39.000
with the global Shannon entropy,

00:58:39.000 --> 00:58:40.910
then this doesn't tell you anything,

00:58:40.910 --> 00:58:43.900
so all three cases are possible.

00:58:43.900 --> 00:58:44.950
If this is,

00:58:44.950 --> 00:58:48.210
the left-hand side is greater
than the right-hand side,

00:58:48.210 --> 00:58:50.970
then only case 1 and 2 is possible,

00:58:50.970 --> 00:58:52.810
and if even the margin,

00:58:52.810 --> 00:58:54.410
the sum of the marginal entropies

00:58:54.410 --> 00:58:57.370
is greater than 2 times the joint entropy,

00:58:57.370 --> 00:58:59.530
then you can say, this
cannot be explained,

00:58:59.530 --> 00:59:01.543
by this or this test, to be this one.

00:59:02.700 --> 00:59:05.020
That's the-

00:59:05.020 --> 00:59:06.743
the strength of this theorem.

00:59:08.200 --> 00:59:09.040
I'm...

00:59:09.040 --> 00:59:10.520
Maybe one final remark,

00:59:10.520 --> 00:59:12.993
I'm not going to talk
about knock-outs anymore,

00:59:14.120 --> 00:59:16.620
but just to-

00:59:16.620 --> 00:59:18.530
refer to this inequit...

00:59:18.530 --> 00:59:21.350
So we continued studying this result,

00:59:21.350 --> 00:59:23.190
which I'm really proud of.

00:59:23.190 --> 00:59:24.600
So it has been published,

00:59:24.600 --> 00:59:27.980
it has been derived by my
former student, Bastian Steudel,

00:59:27.980 --> 00:59:30.160
based on my previous work,

00:59:30.160 --> 00:59:32.110
and we could show, in this paper,

00:59:32.110 --> 00:59:36.094
that it's a sharp bound,
so it cannot get better.

00:59:36.094 --> 00:59:37.383
That's-

00:59:38.220 --> 00:59:41.300
highlighted in this paper,
but in a more recent paper,

00:59:41.300 --> 00:59:43.050
we could show that,

00:59:43.050 --> 00:59:45.110
in most-

00:59:45.110 --> 00:59:46.090
generically...

00:59:46.090 --> 00:59:50.450
So when you sample networks, so to say,

00:59:50.450 --> 00:59:52.760
according to Jeffreys prior,

00:59:52.760 --> 00:59:55.770
then most networks will not be,

00:59:55.770 --> 00:59:58.440
will not allow you to infer common causes.

00:59:58.440 --> 01:00:00.720
So it is sharp,

01:00:00.720 --> 01:00:03.860
in the sense that there
exist situations where you,

01:00:03.860 --> 01:00:06.370
where you reach this upper bound,

01:00:06.370 --> 01:00:09.479
where you have equality
instead of inequality.

01:00:09.479 --> 01:00:12.410
But, these regions where you have this,

01:00:12.410 --> 01:00:16.800
are very tiny, in the space of
all possible causal networks.

01:00:16.800 --> 01:00:18.003
So that's the downside of it.

01:00:19.940 --> 01:00:20.773
And,

01:00:22.660 --> 01:00:25.350
yeah, I think I'll stop here,

01:00:25.350 --> 01:00:27.130
and if there's interest,

01:00:27.130 --> 01:00:28.710
maybe tomorrow I could say more

01:00:28.710 --> 01:00:30.603
about knockout interventions.

01:00:31.470 --> 01:00:34.213
But let me, let me stop here,

01:00:36.270 --> 01:00:38.253
due to the time constraints.

